          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My blood sugar has been over 20 all day and I feel sick. How urgent is that, and what should I be told about diabetic emergencies?",
          "answer": "**Act now. Call your local emergency number or go to the nearest emergency department. Do not wait until tomorrow.**\n\nA blood sugar over 20 that has lasted all day, with nausea, is a combination that the diabetes emergency guidance says needs immediate care. The guidance lists \"nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain\" as a sign to \"call emergency services immediately\" when blood sugar is very high (*Diabetic emergencies: very high and very low blood sugar*, educational summary). An automatic check returned a \"routine\" rating, but that rating is not reliable enough to override this guidance. If you are unsure, treat it as an emergency. If someone is with you, ask them to help you get care now. First, check whether your meter reads in mmol/L or mg/dL, because the two units differ by a large factor (*red-flag-diabetic-emergency*).\n\nCall emergency services immediately, or get someone to take you in, if you have any of these: vomiting or being unable to keep fluids down; stomach or abdominal pain; deep, fast, or laboured breathing; breath that smells fruity or like nail-polish remover; drowsiness, confusion, or difficulty staying awake; severe dehydration, a very dry mouth, or not passing urine; or weakness with fast breathing, especially if you have type 1 diabetes. The guidance says these signs suggest diabetic ketoacidosis (a build-up of acids in the blood when the body lacks enough insulin to use sugar for fuel) or a hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state (very high sugar that leaves the body severely dehydrated). Both \"develop over hours to days and are life-threatening,\" and being sick makes them more likely, because vomiting and poor fluid intake worsen dehydration (*red-flag-diabetic-emergency*, *condition-type-2-diabetes*).\n\nSources differ on how urgent the situation is without any warning signs. The diagnostic guidance says to act now and not wait. The consultation guidance frames it as same-day medical advice, calling your doctor, diabetes team, or urgent line now, and reserves emergency services for the warning signs above. The urgency instruction above follows the diagnostic guidance. Whichever you do, call a doctor or urgent line now and tell them your readings and symptoms. Keep sipping fluids if you can keep them down, and do not stop any medicines without asking a clinician first. If your blood sugar falls very low, or you become confused or lose consciousness, that is also an emergency (*Type 2 diabetes mellitus*, educational summary). The guidance I found does not give treatment steps or a number that means \"call now,\" so the clinician you speak to will need to assess you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
